Don't mistake a command that exited with 259 for a running one.
With -P, xargs looked for finished commands with GetExitCodeProcess()
and took STILL_ACTIVE (259) to mean that a command was still running.
A command that exited with status 259 was never reaped: its handle
stayed signalled, so WaitForMultipleObjects() returned at once every
time and xargs spun at full CPU forever.

Test whether the process handle is signalled instead.

---
 findutils/xargs.c |    5 +++--
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/findutils/xargs.c
+++ b/findutils/xargs.c
@@ -237,8 +237,9 @@
 check_exit_codes:
 	for (i = G.running_procs - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
 		DWORD status = 0;
-		if (!GetExitCodeProcess(G.procs[i], &status) ||
-				status != STILL_ACTIVE) {
+		/* Don't rely on STILL_ACTIVE: a process may exit with 259 */
+		if (WaitForSingleObject(G.procs[i], 0) != WAIT_TIMEOUT) {
+			GetExitCodeProcess(G.procs[i], &status);
 			CloseHandle(G.procs[i]);
 			if (i + 1 < G.running_procs)
 				G.procs[i] = G.procs[G.running_procs - 1];
